--TEST--
A `Core\Socket\Message` cannot be constructed, so the only way to one is a `receive()` inside the isolate an upgrade opened — `rule:concurrency/a-connection-is-a-root-isolate`, `rule:concurrency/an-upgrade-is-spawn-shaped` and `rule:concurrency/a-connection-is-a-loop`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// § 1 makes the socket the isolate's, and § 3 makes `current()` the one way to
// reach it. Both of those are worth nothing if a program can build a
// connection -- or a message that claims to have come from one -- out of
// nothing, so this case pins the other half: a `Core`-owned class has no
// constructor a program can call, and these two are where that matters most.
// A manufactured message is the interesting one, because every reader below
// answers whatever it was built with, and a test double that could forge a
// topic name would be forging the discrimination § 3's loop turns on.
class Chat {
    public static function describe(Core\Socket\Message $msg): string {
        if ($msg->topic() != null) {
            return "a delivery";
        }
        if ($msg->value() != null) {
            return "carrying a value";
        }
        if ($msg->bytes() != null) {
            return "a binary frame";
        }
        if ($msg->text() != null) {
            return "a text frame";
        }
        return "an empty frame";
    }
}

try {
    var $conn = Core\Socket::current();
    var $msg = $conn->receive();
    if ($msg != null) {
        $conn->send(Chat::describe($msg));
        $conn->sendBytes("seen" as bytes);
    }
} catch (LogicError $refused) {
    echo "unreachable\n";
}

var $forged = new Core\Socket\Message();
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0405]: `Core\Socket\Message` has no member named `constructor`
   --> case.nvs:39:15
   |
39 | var $forged = new Core\Socket\Message();
   |               ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^ referenced here

